C = [
("h1", "3. The Place of Propitiation"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 3:21–26 is the passage in which Paul says most plainly what God did in setting Christ forth, and its question is the righteousness of God. It is not first of all a passage about what happens to the sinner. It says twice that God set Christ forth to “declare” his righteousness, and it says why. A reader who reports those clauses has the passage. A reader who adds an account of how the propitiation works has gone beyond it."),
("sub", "3.1 The place of the passage in the letter"),
("p", "The passage follows a long indictment. Paul has shown that Gentiles and Jews are alike under sin, and has concluded that “all the world” may become guilty before God (Romans 3:19) and that “by the law is the knowledge of sin” (Romans 3:20). Then the letter turns: “But now the righteousness of God without the law is manifested, being witnessed by the law and the prophets” (Romans 3:21). The subject of the new section is the righteousness of God, now manifested, and the question it answers is how God can be righteous in dealing with sinners. That is the question the Cross is brought in to answer in this passage."),
("sub", "3.2 Verses 22 to 26 clause by clause"),
("ctbl", "What Romans 3:22–26 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“the righteousness of God which is by faith of Jesus Christ unto all and upon all them that believe”", "The righteousness of God comes by faith of Jesus Christ and is unto all and upon all who believe", "Whether “all” outruns “them that believe”; the sentence bounds it by believing"),
    ("“for there is no difference: For all have sinned, and come short of the glory of God”", "No difference between persons in sin; all fall short", "How sin came to all; that is Romans 5, the subject of Volume 1"),
    ("“Being justified freely by his grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus”", "Justification is free, by grace, through redemption in Christ Jesus", "The price and the company redeemed; the study on redemption carries those"),
    ("“Whom God hath set forth to be a propitiation through faith in his blood”", "God is the one who sets Christ forth; the thing he is set forth as is a propitiation; it is through faith, in his blood", "Whom the propitiation turns away anger from; the clause does not say"),
    ("“to declare his righteousness for the remission of sins that are past, through the forbearance of God”", "The setting forth declares God’s righteousness, with regard to sins that are past, in the forbearance of God", "What those past sins were or how they were dealt with before; the clause gives no account"),
    ("“To declare, I say, at this time his righteousness: that he might be just, and the justifier of him which believeth in Jesus”", "A second declaration, at this time, with a purpose: that God be just and the justifier of the one who believes in Jesus", "How the two are held together; the verse states the purpose, not the method"),
  ], [0.33, 0.34, 0.33]),
("sub", "3.3 “A propitiation”"),
("p", "The King James Version has “propitiation” in Romans 3:25, and the volume uses the word as the translators gave it. In the letter the word stands in a sentence whose subject is God: “Whom God hath set forth.” The sentence does not say that Christ placated God. It says that God set Christ forth. The initiative is God’s. The word is not explained in the verse, and the volume does not explain it further than the verse does. It is a word of the temple’s vocabulary, placed in a letter to believers, and it is joined to “through faith in his blood.”"),
("p", "A reader will ask whether “propitiation” means the turning away of anger or the covering of sin. The objections chapter takes up the question. The volume’s answer here is that the verse states the thing and the purpose, and that the surrounding sentences state the problem: the righteousness of God needed to be declared. The verse does not say that God was angry in the way the word is sometimes used in other places, and it does not say that he was not. It says that he set Christ forth as a propitiation to declare his righteousness."),
("sub", "3.4 “Sins that are past” and “forbearance”"),
("p", "The most carefully worded clause in the passage is “for the remission of sins that are past, through the forbearance of God.” It says that the setting forth bears on sins that are past and that God showed forbearance. It does not say how long the past was, whose sins are meant, or what became of those who lived in that past. The volume reports the clause as written. In particular, it does not use the clause to say what earlier believers were saved by, or that they were saved by the propitiation in the way the readers of this letter are. The clause says that God’s righteousness stood in need of declaration, and that the declaration was made when Christ was set forth. It leaves the rest."),
("p", "There is a reason for this restraint. If the clause were taken to mean that every believer before the Cross was saved by believing in a propitiation, the clause would need to say so, and it does not. It speaks of God’s righteousness, and the verses that follow speak of boasting excluded and of one God who justifies the circumcision by faith and the uncircumcision through faith (Romans 3:27–30). What the believers of earlier times believed is a question about earlier times. It is not answered by borrowing a clause about God’s forbearance."),
("sub", "3.5 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "The problem the passage names is the righteousness of God, which needed to be declared.",
    "The Cross is the place where God set Christ forth as a propitiation, through faith in his blood.",
    "The purpose is stated twice: to declare his righteousness, and that he might be just and the justifier of him which believeth in Jesus.",
    "The result for the sinner is justification, free and by grace, through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.",
    "The passage does not explain how the propitiation does its work, and it does not say what earlier believers were saved by.",
  ]),
("p", "These five statements are what the passage gives. A great deal has been built on them. The next chapters go on to passages in which the Cross is described differently, and the reader is asked to hold the five statements here and not to carry them into Galatians 3 or Colossians 2, where the problem is not the righteousness of God."),
]
